# Mission Reflection

## 1. How does the boot time and setup process of a Docker container compare to installing an operating system on a Virtual Machine?


For me, I noticed that Docker containers are much faster to set up compared to installing an operating system on a Virtual Machine. When using a Virtual Machine, I need to create the virtual machine, assign resources such as RAM and storage, install an operating system, and configure it before I can install and run an application. Because of these steps, the setup can take several minutes or even longer.

With Docker, the process is much simpler. In this activity, I only needed to use commands such as `docker pull nginx` and `docker run` to get the Nginx image and start the web server. I did not need to install another operating system because the container uses the host operating system's kernel. This made the application available much faster.

I also learned that containers generally use fewer resources because they do not include a complete guest operating system like a VM. This makes containers useful when applications need to start quickly and when resources are limited. However, I also understand that VMs and containers are designed for different purposes. Based on my experience in this activity, Docker made the setup of the Nginx web server much faster and easier than setting up a complete Virtual Machine.

