## 2. Why is port mapping (-p 8080:80) necessary when running a web server inside a container?


I learned that port mapping is necessary because the Nginx web server is running inside the Docker container and its port is isolated from the host system. In my activity, Nginx uses port 80 inside the container. If I want to access Nginx from the host, I need to connect a host port to the container's port.

The command I used was `docker run -d --name nginx-server -p 8080:80 nginx`. The `-p 8080:80` means that port 8080 on the host is connected to port 80 inside the container. This allowed me to access the Nginx web server by using `http://localhost:8080`.

I verified that the port mapping was working by running `curl http://localhost:8080` in the KillerCoda terminal. The command returned the HTML code of the Nginx welcome page. This showed me that the request from port 8080 on the host was successfully reaching Nginx inside the container.

Before doing this activity, I did not fully understand how containers could expose their applications to the outside. After performing the command myself, I understood that port mapping acts as a connection between the host and the service running inside the container. It also allows the container to remain isolated while still making the required application accessible.

