## 3. What happens to the data inside a container when you use the docker rm command?


From this activity, I learned that `docker rm` is used to completely remove a stopped container. When I use `docker stop`, the container is only stopped, so I can still see it using `docker ps -a`. However, when I use `docker rm`, the container itself is deleted from Docker.

The data stored in the container's writable layer is also removed when the container is deleted. Because of this, I should not store important or permanent information only inside a container. If I need data to remain available even after deleting a container, I need to use persistent storage such as Docker volumes.

For example, in my activity, I created an Nginx container named `nginx-server`. After testing the web server, I stopped it using `docker stop nginx-server`. I then used `docker ps -a` to verify that the stopped container was still present. After that, I used `docker rm nginx-server` and checked `docker ps -a` again. The container was no longer listed.

This helped me understand the difference between stopping and removing a container. Stopping a container keeps it available for future use, while removing it deletes the container. I learned that container data should be managed properly when working with applications that need permanent information.

